<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.8.4: Writing Equations Given Dimensions of Shapes</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00182</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.8.4: Writing Equations Given Dimensions of Shapes</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ee4015e2-0aba-4f49-a6a9-c7e19756795f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
      <title>Launch</title>
        <para>Give students continued access to calculators.</para>
      <title>Student Activity</title>
        <para>The perimeter of a rectangle is 48 centimeters. The relationship between the length, the width, and the perimeter of the rectangle can be described with the equation: \( 2 \cdot \text{length} +2 \cdot \text{width}=48 \).</para>
        <para>Find the length, in centimeters, if the width is:</para>
        <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
          <item>10 centimeters
          </item>
        </list>
        <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
        <para>\(2L + 2(10) = 48\)</para>

        <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
          <item>3.6 centimeters
          </item>
        </list>
        <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
        <para>\(2L + 2(3.6) = 48\)</para>

        <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
          <item>
            \( w \) centimeters
          </item>
        </list>
        <para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
        <para>
          \(2L + 2(w) = 48\)</para>
      
    
  
</section>
</content>
</document>
